# ── Database roles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three roles are provisioned by scripts/db/pg-init-roles.sh. They are NOT
# interchangeable: RLS enforcement depends on which one you connect as.

# The application role. DML only, NOT the table owner, so Row Level Security is
# enforced. Every request-path query uses this.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://fgmp_app:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5433/fgmp?schema=public

# Prisma connection-pool guardrails for hosted PostgreSQL plans with a small
# connection allowance (e.g. 15 concurrent connections). Automatically defaults
# to connection_limit=1 on serverless/edge runtimes (Cloudflare Workers) and 3 on long-lived
# app servers. Shared privileged clients default to 1 in both environments.
# Explicit connection_limit / pool_timeout / connect_timeout URL parameters
# always win. Set these only when you have measured a need to override defaults.
# FGMP_DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT=1
# FGMP_DB_POOL_TIMEOUT=15

# The owner/migration role. Used ONLY for work that cannot be tenant-scoped:
# Prisma migrations, seedIfNeeded(), and the scripts in scripts/ that create or
# repair data across tenants.
#
# Required because RLS is FORCE-enabled on every tenant-carrying table, so a
# write with no app.tenant_id in scope fails its WITH CHECK:
#     ERROR: new row violates row-level security policy for table "User"
#
# SECURITY: this role is a SUPERUSER and bypasses RLS entirely. Do not use it as
# DATABASE_URL, and do not give it to the web app container unless seeding still
# runs on the request path.
FGMP_MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://fgmp_migrate:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5433/fgmp?schema=public

# BYPASSRLS (not superuser) for ops tooling: pg_dump, support investigations.
# MUST NOT be used as DATABASE_URL for the web app.
#
# ── WHY LEAVING THIS EMPTY BREAKS SIGN-IN ───────────────────────────────────
# The login lookup is the one query that cannot be tenant-scoped: you cannot know
# the tenant until you have found the user, so "email → user" is inherently
# cross-tenant. src/lib/auth-identity.ts therefore runs it on THIS credential.
# With it unset, the lookup falls back to DATABASE_URL (fgmp_app), `"User"` is
# FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, the query returns zero rows for EVERY account, and
# every login returns 401 — indistinguishable in the browser from a wrong
# password, with no error in the response. If sign-in fails with nothing else
# wrong, check this variable first.
FGMP_OPS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://fgmp_ops:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5433/fgmp?schema=public

# Refuse to start if DATABASE_URL does not use the `fgmp_app` role. The table
# owner bypasses RLS, so an owner-role connection would silently disable every
# tenant isolation guarantee. Enforced twice: src/lib/db.ts on first import, and
# src/instrumentation.ts at boot (exit 1) so the refusal does not wait for the
# first request. Hosted poolers that prefix the role with the project ref
# (`fgmp_app.<ref>`) are recognised.
# Set to 0 ONLY for local development against a database with no policies.
FGMP_REQUIRE_RLS=1

# ── Simulated telemetry (Phase 2) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# OFF by default. When unset, the engine fabricates NOTHING: the platform shows
# real ingested data, and a genuine empty state when there is none.
#
# Set to 1 ONLY in development, to generate tank levels, generator temperatures
# and fuel transactions for a demo. This writes plausible-looking numbers into
# the same tables real telemetry uses, which is exactly why it must never run
# in production — the guard in src/lib/fgmp/simulation.ts refuses when
# NODE_ENV=production REGARDLESS of this value, so a stray "1" here is not a way
# to switch it on in prod.
#
# The simulation is reproducible: the same seed replays the same run.
FGMP_SIMULATION=0

# Seed for that reproducible run. Any integer. Changing it changes every
# generated value. Ignored when FGMP_SIMULATION is not 1.
FGMP_SIM_SEED=20240617

# ── MFA secret encryption ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TOTP secrets are stored encrypted (AES-GCM). Without this key the MFA
# endpoints refuse with a configuration error — the app runs, but nobody can
# enrol a second factor. Set it ONCE and keep it stable: changing it makes every
# stored secret undecryptable and users must re-enrol. Generate with:
#     openssl rand -base64 48
FGMP_MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Application secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 32   (or -hex 32 for API keys)
# Device ingest uses tenant-scoped API keys with write scope. The bridge can
# receive a JSON map from tenant IDs to raw keys via IOT_API_KEYS (see docs).
IOT_API_KEYS=
# Service token the iot-bridge presents on every /api/fgmp/realtime/snapshot
# poll. That endpoint stays after the Apinator migration (the bridge cannot
# hold a NextAuth browser session), so this shared secret stays with it.
REALTIME_SERVICE_TOKEN=
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Apinator (hosted realtime) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Replaces the retired self-hosted socket.io mini-service (mini-services/
# realtime, :3003 behind Caddy /socket.io). APP_ID / KEY / SECRET come from the
# Apinator dashboard — they are account-scoped credentials, NOT openssl
# output. CLUSTER selects the data-plane region ("eu" or "us") and must match
# the region the dashboard app was created in.
APINATOR_APP_ID=
APINATOR_KEY=
APINATOR_SECRET=
APINATOR_CLUSTER=eu

# Browser pair for the WebSocket client. NEXT_PUBLIC_ vars are inlined into
# the client bundle at BUILD time — set them before `next build` (or pass
# them as Docker build args), not only in the runtime environment. The app
# key here is the public one; it only identifies the app, while channel
# subscriptions for private-* channels are signed server-side by the app's
# realtime auth routes using APINATOR_SECRET.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APINATOR_APP_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_APINATOR_CLUSTER=eu
# ── Rate limiting (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The limiter is shared through Redis so limits hold across replicas. With no
# REDIS_URL every limit falls back to an in-process map — still enforced, but
# multiplied by the number of replicas, and reported as degraded
# (fgmp_rate_limit_degraded=1). Login fails CLOSED when the store is
# unavailable; ingest fails OPEN (dropping telemetry is worse than losing abuse
# protection there). Set FGMP_RATE_LIMIT_REDIS=0 to disable Redis explicitly.
REDIS_URL=
# FGMP_RATE_LIMIT_REDIS=0

# ── Observability ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Prisma query logging floods production logs; keep it off and enable briefly
# only when diagnosing a query. /api/fgmp/metrics exposes Prometheus text.
# DEBUG_PRISMA=0

# ── Deploy-time seeding ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Seeding is NOT automatic. After `prisma migrate deploy`, seed a fresh
# environment explicitly:
#     docker compose --profile seed run --rm fgmp-seed
# It is idempotent and exits non-zero if it produces no sites.

# ── Published demo credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The seeders create six accounts with passwords that are in this repository
# (Super@2026 / Admin@2026). With NODE_ENV=production and this unset, a login
# that verifies with one of those published passwords is REFUSED — the check runs
# after bcrypt succeeds, returns the same null a wrong password does, and logs the
# real reason server-side. That is fail-closed on purpose: a deployment that
# forgets to rotate should not be reachable with credentials anyone can read.
#
# Set to 1 ONLY for a demonstration deployment. The supported fix is to rotate the
# passwords (bun run scripts/set-password.ts <email> <new-password>), not to set
# this.
# FGMP_ALLOW_DEMO_CREDENTIALS=1
